<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of the Seven Tenths</h1><p class="st-meta">1628 CE – 1798 CE · capital Sion</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Alpine peasant republic of the upper Rhône valley, an ally of the Swiss Confederacy that ruled itself through seven communal districts.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The communities (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenden">Zenden</a>) of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-valais">Upper Valais</a> had long contested power with the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sion-switzerland">Sion</a>. After decades of struggle, the Zenden forced Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildebrand-jost">Hildebrand Jost</a> to abandon most of his temporal rights in 1628 and finally in 1634, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valais">Valais</a> became a republic of seven tenths: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goms">Goms</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brig-switzerland">Brig</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visp">Visp</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raron">Raron</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuk">Leuk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierre">Sierre</a> and Sion.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">Sovereignty</a> lay with the Diet (Landrat), in which each tenth sent delegates, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> kept a largely honorary role. The republic was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zugewandter-ort">Zugewandter Ort</a>, an associate of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy</a>, and governed the French-speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-valais">Lower Valais</a> as a subject territory. Its economy rested on Alpine farming and trade over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simplon-pass">Simplon</a> pass, dominated by families such as the Stockalper of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brig-switzerland">Brig</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-valais">Lower Valais</a> rebelled in 1798 and French troops entered the country. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valais">Valais</a> was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a> in 1798, later becoming the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rhodanic-republic">Rhodanic Republic</a>, a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>, and in 1815 a Swiss canton.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-the-seven-tenths.jpg" alt="Republic of the Seven Tenths" width="320" height="322"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown author Vector: SpinnerLaserzthe2nd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CHE_Valais_1802_Flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1628 CE – 1798 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sion</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Alemannic German, Franco-Provençal</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Switzerland</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Valais, Sieben Zenden</dd></dl></aside></div>
